# Map-Not-Route Briefing Convention

## Setup

Use this template's structure directly when briefing an auditor, reviewer, or dispatched checker
(e.g. Frank). It is a **practice-only convention** — a discipline you follow when composing a
briefing message — not an installed mechanism. No hook, gate, or automated enforcement checks
that a briefing conforms to this shape (see Preamble below for the specific enforcement caveat).

Source-of-record for this file lives in the `agent-rig` repo (this repo), per DDR-014's
author≠install-location pattern — edit it here, redeploy to `~/.claude/templates/` if/when this
convention is propagated.

---

## Preamble — scope and reconciliation

**What this is:** a briefing-composition discipline for the agent *dispatching* a checker. Give
the checker the **map** (objective, architecture, what's claimed) and withhold the **route** (your
own method, checklist, or suspicions) — a method handed over is a lens handed over, and it caps
the checker's ceiling at yours. This is the same principle stated in the global CLAUDE.md's
Research Data Integrity rule 3 ("give the map, never the route").

**Reconciliation against DDR-INDEX backlog item 25** (`docs/specs/agent-rig-ddrs/00-DDR-INDEX.md`,
"Prevention layer: gap-lens pre-checks at the DOER + DISPATCHER layer, not just the checker"):
this template and item 25 are **kept separate, not folded in** — they operate on different
architectural surfaces. Item 25 is a **prevention-layer** proposal: embedding gap-lens pre-checks
into doer/dispatcher agent prompts *before* a handoff happens, so a shared-well corruption never
gets produced in the first place. This template is a **detection-layer** convention: it shapes
what a briefing contains *at* the moment of dispatch to a checker, so the checker's independence is
preserved once a handoff does happen. Item 25 remains its own backlog entry, unresolved and not
started as of this writing — this template does not implement, supersede, or substitute for it.

**Enforcement caveat (Frank, `ALPHA-REPORT-REVIEW.md`, per this sprint's Slice 5 mandatory note):**
DDR-0009 (hook-based/automated enforcement of briefing discipline) is confirmed
`PROPOSED / QUEUED (not started)`. No hook, PreToolUse gate, or other automated mechanism enforces
that a briefing conforms to this template. Following this convention is a manual discipline
practiced by the dispatching agent — nothing currently checks or blocks a non-conforming briefing.

---

## Objective

State the checker's task in one or two sentences: what verdict or judgment is being asked for, and
against what standard. Name the artifact(s) under review by path. Do not describe how you arrived
at your own belief about the artifact's correctness — only what question the checker is being
asked to answer.

## Architecture

Describe where the relevant things live: file paths, component boundaries, how pieces connect,
what changed and what didn't. Give enough structural context that the checker can navigate the
artifact independently — this is orientation, not verification guidance. Do not include your own
checklist, test plan, or the specific sequence of checks you performed or would perform.

## What's Claimed

State plainly what is being asserted as true or complete, and by whom (e.g. "the producer claims
Slice 5 is done-when-complete"). Distinguish claims from evidence already independently verified,
if any. This section exists so the checker knows what to test against — not so the checker adopts
the claimant's own confidence.

---

**Structurally omitted by design:** this template has no checklist, method, or suspicions section.
If a briefing drafted from this template starts to include "things I already checked" or "things
I'm worried about," move that content out before sending — a checker primed with the author's own
suspicions is no longer verifying independently.
